    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d181-a5">

                <pb facs="0536" n="1166" sameAs="frus:frus1873p1v2/pg1166"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 5.—Translation.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p>“XLV. The embassadors of the very magnificent Emperor of France, as well as his
                    consuls, will employ such dragomans as they please, and such janissaries as they
                    choose, without any one having a right to compel them to employ those whom they
                    should not like.”</p>
                <p>“XLVI. Of the servants <hi rendition="#italic">rayas</hi><anchor n="*"
                        xml:id="d181-f1"/>, or subjects to my Sublime Porte, who may be in the
                    service of the embassador in his palace, fifteen only shall be exempt of
                    taxation, and cannot be annoyed on that subject.” (Treaty of 1740 between France
                    and the Porte.)</p>
                <p>“I. Consuls may employ Ottoman subjects, as privileged protégés, to the following
                    numbers:</p>
                <p>“Consuls-general and the consuls of seats of provinces, four dragomen and four
                    yassakdjis.</p>
                <p>“Consuls under a consul-general, three dragomen and three yassakdjis.</p>
                <p>“Vice-consuls and consular agents, two dragomen and two vassakdjis.” (Rules from
                    the Ottoman foreign office concerning the number of Ottoman subjects whom
                    foreign consuls can protect, issued in August, 1863.)</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d181-f1" n="*" xml:id="d181-f1-content">
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Rayas</hi> are the Christian Ottoman subjects.</p>
            </note>
